<h1 class="wp-block-heading has-text-color has-fluid-7-xl-font-size" style="color:#ffffff;letter-spacing:-0.02em;line-height:1.02">Saltmarsh Field Station</h1>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph {"style":{"color":{"text":"#ffffff"}},"fontSize":"xl"} -->
<p class="has-text-color has-xl-font-size" style="color:#ffffff">Blackshore Point, Suffolk — completed spring 2025 for the Deben Estuary Trust</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph --></div>
<!-- /wp:group --></div>
<!-- /wp:group --></div></div>
<!-- /wp:cover -->

<!-- wp:group {"align":"full","style":{"spacing":{"padding":{"top":"var:preset|spacing|9","right":"var:preset|spacing|5","bottom":"var:preset|spacing|9","left":"var:preset|spacing|5"},"blockGap":"var:preset|spacing|9"}},"layout":{"type":"constrained"}} -->
<div class="wp-block-group alignfull" style="padding-top:var(--wp--preset--spacing--9);padding-right:var(--wp--preset--spacing--5);padding-bottom:var(--wp--preset--spacing--9);padding-left:var(--wp--preset--spacing--5)"><!-- wp:group {"style":{"spacing":{"blockGap":"var:preset|spacing|8"}},"layout":{"type":"constrained"}} -->
<div class="wp-block-group"><!-- wp:paragraph {"style":{"typography":{"lineHeight":"1.6"}},"fontSize":"xl"} -->
<p class="has-xl-font-size" style="line-height:1.6">The trust has counted birds on this stretch of marsh since 1978, mostly from the back of a van parked on the sea wall. They asked for somewhere to put eight people, four microscopes and a freezer full of samples, on a site that floods twice a day and will be open water within a lifetime.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:group {"style":{"spacing":{"blockGap":"var:preset|spacing|4"}},"layout":{"type":"constrained"}} -->
<div class="wp-block-group"><!-- wp:paragraph {"style":{"typography":{"fontWeight":"600","letterSpacing":"0.12em","textTransform":"uppercase"}},"textColor":"muted-foreground","fontSize":"xs"} -->
<p class="has-muted-foreground-color has-text-color has-xs-font-size" style="font-weight:600;letter-spacing:0.12em;text-transform:uppercase">The site</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":2,"style":{"typography":{"letterSpacing":"-0.01em"}},"fontSize":"3-xl"} -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading has-3-xl-font-size" style="letter-spacing:-0.01em">Building on ground that will not stay</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph {"fontSize":"lg"} -->
<p class="has-lg-font-size">Blackshore Point is a spit of grazing marsh behind a shingle bank that the sea has been rearranging since the last serious breach in 2013. The environment agency's own maps show the bank gone by the 2060s and the whole field under tidal water. Designing a permanent building there would have been dishonest, so we designed one that can be unbolted.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph {"fontSize":"lg"} -->
<p class="has-lg-font-size">Everything sits a metre and a half above the highest recorded tide on fourteen helical steel piles, wound into the clay without a single pour of concrete. No pile is more than four metres from its neighbour, which kept the sections light enough to be craned in from the lane in a day and a half, and which means the whole frame can be wound back out and re-set further inland when the water arrives.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph --></div>
<!-- /wp:group -->

<!-- wp:group {"style":{"spacing":{"blockGap":"var:preset|spacing|4"}},"layout":{"type":"constrained"}} -->
<div class="wp-block-group"><!-- wp:paragraph {"style":{"typography":{"fontWeight":"600","letterSpacing":"0.12em","textTransform":"uppercase"}},"textColor":"muted-foreground","fontSize":"xs"} -->
<p class="has-muted-foreground-color has-text-color has-xs-font-size" style="font-weight:600;letter-spacing:0.12em;text-transform:uppercase">The building</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":2,"style":{"typography":{"letterSpacing":"-0.01em"}},"fontSize":"3-xl"} -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading has-3-xl-font-size" style="letter-spacing:-0.01em">A long room, a warm room and a shutter</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph {"fontSize":"lg"} -->
<p class="has-lg-font-size">The plan is one thirty-two metre deck under a single shallow roof. The eastern two thirds is the working room — benches along the glazed wall, a wet lab and sample store behind, and a hatch at the far end where boats hand up crates without anyone going down the steps. The western third is insulated, heated by a small air source unit and used for writing up, eating and the two nights a week when someone stays over.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph {"fontSize":"lg"} -->
<p class="has-lg-font-size">Between them runs a full-height larch shutter that folds flat against the wall in summer and closes the warm room off in February. It is the only moving part in the building and the researchers operate it by hand, which was deliberate: nothing here depends on a control system that a volunteer committee would have to maintain from thirty miles away.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph --></div>
<!-- /wp:group --></div>
<!-- /wp:group -->

